1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,790
Have you ever taken a taxi?
你坐过出租车吗？

2
00:00:03,790 --> 00:00:15,640
In the past, doing so meant finding one, flagging it down and telling the driver where you wanted to go, paying, and possibly, adding a tip.
在过去，坐出租车意味着要找到一辆车，挥手拦下（flagging down），然后告诉司机你要去的地方，付款，而且可能还要给小费。

3
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:21,330
And while this still happens, it’s less common than it used to be.
虽然这种情况仍然存在，但不像过去那么常见了。

4
00:00:21,330 --> 00:00:26,620
These days, it’s much easier to open a ride-hailing app.
如今，打开一个叫车应用程序要容易得多。

5
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:29,700
All of the boring stuff gets taken care of.
所有那些麻烦的事情都有人处理。

6
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:32,210
It’s a perfect solution!
这似乎是个完美的解决方案！

7
00:00:32,210 --> 00:00:35,090
Except that it isn’t.
只可惜事实并非如此。

8
00:00:35,090 --> 00:00:49,930
A recent report from urban transport consultant Bruce Schaller, who spent the last four years studying traffic flow in New York, concluded that the use of these app-based journeys is causing traffic jams.
城市交通顾问布鲁斯・沙勒（Bruce Schaller）在过去四年里一直在研究纽约的交通流量，他在最近的一份报告中得出结论，使用这些基于应用程序的出行方式正在造成交通拥堵。

9
00:00:49,930 --> 00:01:00,810
The 81% increase in these city-based rides means that there are now five times more of them than the iconic yellow taxicab.
这些通过应用程序叫车的城市出行次数增加了81%，这意味着现在这类出行的数量是标志性的黄色出租车的五倍。

10
00:01:00,810 --> 00:01:06,080
“It’s the shift in patronage from public transport,” he told the BBC.
“人们不再使用（惠顾）公共交通，”他告诉英国广播公司（BBC）。

11
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:11,970
For whatever reason, people decide that it’s just too much hassle.
不管出于什么原因，人们认为乘坐公共交通太麻烦了。

12
00:01:11,970 --> 00:01:20,200
“That means moving people from larger vehicles into smaller ones, which means more vehicles to move the same people.
“这意味着人们从大型交通工具转向小型交通工具，也就意味着需要更多的车辆来运送同样数量的人。

13
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:23,010
Therefore, more traffic.”
因此，交通会更加拥堵。”

14
00:01:23,010 --> 00:01:39,955
In addition, the sheer number of cars now looking for a commuter means that they spend 45% of their time empty, just cruising for a fare, and inadvertently clogging up the city’s infrastructure.
此外，现在大量的汽车在寻找通勤乘客，这意味着它们45%的时间都是空车，只是在巡游揽客，并且在不经意间堵塞了城市的基础设施。

15
00:01:39,955 --> 00:01:53,140
“They are out there in force at the worst possible times,” said Prof Christo Wilson, a computer scientist at Northeastern University, who has also studied the services.
“它们在最糟糕的时候大量出现，”东北大学（Northeastern University）的计算机科学家克里斯托・威尔逊（Christo Wilson）教授说，他也在研究这些出行服务。

16
00:01:53,140 --> 00:02:00,740
He refers to Uber’s traffic data, which ‘perfectly matches the peaks for the rush hour.’
他提到优步（Uber）的交通数据，这些数据“与高峰时段的流量高峰完美匹配”。

17
00:02:00,740 --> 00:02:11,000
He also adds that the low fares and convenience mean that people are ordering rides to go from A to B when they wouldn’t normally.
他还补充说，低廉的票价和便利性意味着人们在平时不会打车的时候也会叫车从A地前往B地。

18
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,010
Both help contribute to the gridlock.
这两个因素都导致了交通堵塞。

19
00:02:15,010 --> 00:02:19,880
So what can be done to stop traffic from backing up or bottlenecking?
那么，要怎么做才能阻止出现交通堵塞或瓶颈呢？

20
00:02:19,880 --> 00:02:25,200
One solution involves combining ride-hailing with sharing.
一个解决方案是将叫车服务与拼车相结合。

21
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:34,680
Many ride-hailing companies, such as Uber, Didi Chuxing in China and Ola in India enable customers to carpool.
许多叫车公司，比如优步、中国的滴滴出行和印度的奥拉（Ola），都让客户可以拼车。

22
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:40,220
This allows travellers who share similar destinations to share the journey.
这使得有相似目的地的旅客可以共享行程。

23
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:48,750
The app enables an en route pickup, which means more streamlined traffic, and less time spent with an empty vehicle.
应用程序支持途中接客，这意味着更加顺畅（流线型）的交通，空车行驶的时间也更少。

24
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:53,570
Could this decongest the world’s jam-packed roads?
这能疏通世界上拥堵不堪的道路吗？

25
00:02:53,570 --> 00:02:56,030
It is certainly one solution.
这当然是一个解决方案。

26
00:02:56,030 --> 00:03:02,180
But maybe we shouldn’t be so quick to dismiss the more traditional public transport options.
但也许我们不应该这么快就否定更传统的公共交通选择。

27
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:09,610
"Congestion is a really complicated issue," said Andrew Salzberg, head of Uber's transport policy.
优步交通政策负责人安德鲁・萨尔茨伯格（Andrew Salzberg）说：“交通拥堵是一个非常复杂的问题。”

28
00:03:09,610 --> 00:03:16,340
But, “There is no more efficient way to move people around than public transport," he added.
但是，“没有比公共交通更有效的运送人们的方式了，”他补充道。

29
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:20,980
So don’t do away with that bus pass just yet.
所以，先别扔掉你的公交卡。
